        'A falling object speeds up until drag balances its weight. Setting ½ρC_DAv_t² = mg gives the terminal velocity v_t = √(2mg/(ρC_DA)). Heavy, compact objects fall fast; light, broad ones like feathers and parachutes fall slowly.',
        'A jet of fluid carries momentum at the rate ρQv (mass flow rate × speed). Changing that momentum requires a force: a fire hose nozzle recoils with F = ρQv, and a rocket or jet engine produces thrust equal to the momentum it throws backward per second.',
        'Lift on a wing comes from a pressure difference between its lower and upper surfaces, created by the wing turning the airflow downward. Real flows also separate from surfaces and form wakes; the dimples on a golf ball trigger turbulence that delays separation, shrinking the wake and reducing drag.',
